Q: Why does resolve(Promise) lag behind interleaved promise chains before catching up? A: <p>when we have,</p>
<pre><code>
    var thenable = new Promise( function (resolve, reject){
        resolve( '1' );
    } );

    var follow = new Promise( function (resolve, reject){
        resolve( thenable );
    });

    follow.then((a) =&gt; {
        console.log('follow 1', a);
        return 1;
    });
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Phase 1 : Synchronous</strong><br />
<strong>Action</strong>: &quot;thenable&quot; is created and resolved to '1'.<br />
<strong>The Unwrapping Logic</strong>: The engine detects that &quot;thenable&quot; in <code>resolve(thenable)</code> is a Promise. As Per the spec, &quot;follow&quot; cannot settle until thenable settles, presence  of <code>resolve(thenable)</code> queues a PromiseResolveThenableJob<br />
<strong>Scheduling</strong>: The engine schedules PromiseResolveThenableJob in Microtask Queue which contains resolution task <code>() =&gt;  {thenable.then(resolve)}</code>.</p>
<p>the synchronous pseudo code can be roughly interpreted as :</p>
<pre><code>
    new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {
        [PromiseResolveThenableJob].then(() =&gt; {
            thenable.then(resolve);
        });
    });
</code></pre>
<p>Or more precisely the PromiseResolveThenableJob is not itself a Promise — it's a plain microtask job.</p>
<pre><code>    microtaskQueue.enqueue(() =&gt; {
        thenable.then(resolveFollow);
    });
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Action</strong>: The PromiseResolveThenableJob schedules the job <code>() =&gt; {thenable.then(resolve)}</code> in microtask queue<br />
<strong>Subscriber Registration</strong>: <code>follow.then(...)</code> is called. Since &quot;follow&quot; is still pending, the callback is stored in &quot;follow&quot;'s internal <code>[[PromiseFulfillReactions]]</code> list.</p>
<p><strong>Phase 2 : MICROTASK TICK 1</strong><br />
<strong>Task</strong>: Execute the NewPromiseResolveThenableJob<br />
<strong>Action</strong>: This runs the resolution code <code>() =&gt; {thenable.then(resolve)}</code> which was pushed by PromiseResolveThenableJob to microtask queue<br />
<strong>Effect</strong>: Since &quot;thenable&quot; is already fulfilled, this <code>.then()</code> schedules another microtask to execute the <code>resolve()</code> of the &quot;follow&quot; promise or subscribes to &quot;thenable&quot; if its not fulfilled</p>
<p>Executed Code:</p>
<pre><code>
      () =&gt; {
          thenable.then(resolve);
      }
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Phase 3 : MICROTASK TICK 2</strong><br />
<strong>Task</strong>: Execute the reaction scheduled by <code>thenable.then(...)</code> which is <code>resolve()</code> of the &quot;follow&quot; promise in the microtask queue<br />
<strong>Effect</strong>: &quot;follow&quot; transitions from pending to fulfilled.<br />
Scheduling: This fulfillment now schedules the callbacks attached to follow into the Microtask Queue.</p>
<p><strong>Phase 4 : MICROTASK TICK 3</strong><br />
<strong>Task</strong>: Execute the <code>.then()</code> callback attached to follow.<br />
<strong>Action</strong>: <code>console.log('follow 1', '1')</code> is executed.</p>

Q: JaCoCo indicates missed branch on two-part RegEx matches/find which is proved to be covered between multiple unit tests A: <p>Your email validation regex is incorrect. See <a href="https://emailregex.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">emailregex.com</a> for a better regex and a general treatise on why this is mostly a silly idea. If you wish to validate an email, send an email to it with a link with a token in it that the receiver must click.</p>
<p>Your code is also broken: <code>find()</code> is not <strong>idempotent</strong>, and your code is written as if it is - you call <code>uuidFinder.find()</code> a whole bunch of times, the code clearly written assuming that this call will return the same result every time you call it which is not true - <code>find()</code> changes the state of the matcher, every <code>find()</code> call finds the next. Given that presumably there's only one UUID in there, the first <code>find()</code> call says <code>true</code>, all further calls say <code>false</code>.</p>
<p>The correct code is to call <code>matcher.matches()</code> <strong>just once</strong>, and the same for <code>uuidMatcher.find()</code>:</p>
<pre><code>boolean uuidPresent = uuidMatcher.find();
boolean emailValid = matcher.matches();

if (emailValid &amp;&amp; uuidPresent) {
  ....
}
...
</code></pre>
<p>If wonder if your coverage issues exist <em>because</em> of these errors; I think so. Fix your code, rerun, I bet the problem you're asking about goes away.</p>
<p>Trivially, a UUID in an email address is a perfectly valid email address. Ordinarily I'd say that wanting to exclude them is therefore misguided. But, you know the domain where this code is meant to be best. However, your names are off. A method named <code>validate()</code> in a type named <code>EmailValidationService</code> do not in any way indicate the 'haha! Surprise!!!' that it also weirdly checked for UUIDs as well. The name <code>EmailValidationService</code> is somewhat misleading. But this is style advice, and like all style advice, just be aware of it, if you feel that given the context of this code the names are the best compromise, then ignore this advice.</p>

Q: What does the &#39;L&#39; in the beginning even mean? A: <p><code>&quot;Game Window Class&quot;</code> is a string literal mostly analogous to an array of standard 8-bit characters (char) with a null char. It's more or less the same as this declaration as you likely know</p>
<pre><code>const char name[] = {
    71, 97, 109, 101, 32,         &quot;Game &quot;
    67, 108, 97, 115, 115, 32,    &quot;Class &quot;
    87, 105, 110, 100, 111, 119,  &quot;Window&quot;
    0                             &lt;null&gt;
};
</code></pre>
<p><code>L&quot;Game Window Glass&quot;</code> is analogous to a array of wide chars with a null wide char at the end. On Windows, wchar_t maps to an unsigned short. On Mac, it maps to a 32-bit int.  Windows wide strings are 16-bit usually interpreted to be UTF-16 (little endian) strings.  On Mac, they are usually interpreted as UTF-32 strings.</p>
<pre><code>// typedef unsigned short wchar_t;

const wchar_t name[] = {
    71, 97, 109, 101, 32,
    67, 108, 97, 115, 115, 32,
    87, 105, 110, 100, 111, 119,
    0
};
</code></pre>
